---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Divider from '../components/Divider.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';
---

<Layout title="Law Firm Technology Consulting" description="Wichita law firm technology consulting: IT issue tracking, reporting dashboards, websites, and discovery tools in development, from an independent local consultant.">

<!-- ── PAGE HERO ── -->
<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-grid"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-glow"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <div class="page-eyebrow fade-up d1">
      <span class="eyebrow-num">// Legal Tech</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="page-headline fade-up d2">
      Law Firm<br>Technology Consulting<br><em>in Wichita</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="page-sub fade-up d3">
      Practical technology for small law firms: IT tracking, reporting dashboards, and websites,
      with discovery and evidence tools in development. Built by an independent local consultant.
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-meta fade-up d4">
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Wichita, KS</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Independent Consultancy</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Free Scoping Call</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SUMMARY + CONTENTS ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="lp-intro">
      <div class="lp-card reveal">
        <div class="lp-card-label">// The short version</div>
        <ul class="lp-summary-list">
          <li><strong>Who it's for</strong>Small law firms in Wichita, KS that don't have a full in-house IT department.</li>
          <li><strong>What you get</strong>IT issue tracking, reporting dashboards, and websites today, with discovery and evidence tools in development. The tools are built by DataForge.</li>
          <li><strong>Independence</strong>DataForge is a separate, independent company, and nothing here speaks for any law firm.</li>
          <li><strong>How it starts</strong>A free scoping call, then interviews with a few of your staff.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="lp-card-label">// In this page</div>
        <ol class="lp-toc">
          <li><a href="#independent">Independent, and how I work</a></li>
          <li><a href="#it">When nobody owns IT</a></li>
          <li><a href="#caselens">Reviewing discovery data: CaseLens</a></li>
          <li><a href="#evidence">Evidence processing</a></li>
          <li><a href="#reporting">Reporting, websites, and a first engagement</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 01 INDEPENDENT, AND WHO I AM ── -->
<div id="independent" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">01</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Independent, and<br><em>How I Work</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        DataForge is a separate, independent company, and I work as a law firm technology
        consultant here in Wichita. Nothing on this page speaks for any law firm, and no firm is
        named here.
      </p>
      <p>
        My method is diagnostic first. I pull a system apart, whether it's a reporting workflow, an
        intake process, or an automation that has grown brittle, and find where the original design
        ends and the workarounds begin. The workarounds are usually the problem. The same approach
        applies outside law, and I work as a business systems consultant in Wichita for other small
        businesses too. The aim is always the same: find the inefficiency, build the solution, and
        make it stick.
      </p>
      <p>
        On confidentiality, here is how I work as an independent consultant engaged by a firm. I
        don't upload or share information that could reveal the firm's clients. I only access what
        a task requires, and I don't need case content to build or fix a system. If an engagement
        involves regulated data, such as health information, we set up the right agreements with
        the firm first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 02 WHEN NOBODY OWNS IT ── -->
<div id="it" class="lp-anchor" style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">02</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">When Nobody<br><em>Owns IT</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Many small law firms don't have an in-house IT department. IT Logger was built for a law
        firm's internal IT support, in a setting where a contract vendor was called in for major
        failures and nothing tracked the smaller problems. When a printer jammed or a workstation
        lost its network connection, the fix depended on who happened to be nearby. Each issue
        looked like a one-off, because nobody kept a record.
      </p>
      <p>
        IT Logger is a small ticket tracker: a log of who reported what, from which
        department, what type of issue it was, its priority, and when it was logged and closed. The
        intake form takes under two minutes, and a short dropdown for issue type forces
        categorization without adding friction. I run it myself. It runs locally on one machine,
        using Python, Flask, and SQLite, with no licenses and no outside services. Only I can see
        it, and the log data stays on that machine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once issues were logged, a pattern appeared. Printer trouble was the biggest single
        category, and it clustered around the same shared printers. The training questions
        clustered around a document-management software rollout that had no structured handoff.
        What looked like random friction was a systemic gap, and individual annoyances turned out
        to have fixable causes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="lp-figure reveal">
      <img src="/images/tools/itlogger-dashboard.webp" alt="IT Logger dashboard showing total, open and resolved ticket counts and average resolution time, a donut chart of issue types, and a bar chart of monthly ticket volume over six months." width="1600" height="535" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      <figcaption>Dashboard with ticket totals, issue type distribution and monthly volume</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 03 CASELENS ── -->
<div id="caselens" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">03</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Reviewing Discovery Data:<br><em>CaseLens</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Discovery is the stage where both sides hand over case data. CaseLens, built by DataForge,
        is a Windows desktop app for reviewing exported message data. A reviewer can browse and
        search the messages and export findings to CSV, a plain spreadsheet format. Cases open
        read-only, so the source data can't be changed by accident.
      </p>
      <p>
        CaseLens is currently in development. It is planned to be available to other businesses to
        purchase once it is ready, and I'm not giving a date yet. You can read more in the
        <a href="/work/caselens/">CaseLens write-up</a> and on the <a href="/tools/">Tools page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 04 EVIDENCE PROCESSING ── -->
<div id="evidence" class="lp-anchor" style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">04</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Evidence<br><em>Processing</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        The Evidence Processing Suite (working name), also built by DataForge, is a companion
        toolset for evidence files. It pulls still frames out of video, reads the text in images
        and screenshots using OCR (optical character recognition), and lays frames out in contact
        sheets: PDF grids of frames with timestamps, designed for quick attorney review instead of
        opening hundreds of files one by one.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also records a SHA-256 hash for evidence files. A hash is a digital fingerprint that
        changes if a file is altered, so you can check later whether a file is unchanged. I make no
        claim about what any court will accept; that's a question for counsel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Like CaseLens, the suite is in development and is planned to be available to other
        businesses to purchase once it is ready.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 05 REPORTING, WEBSITES, FIRST ENGAGEMENT ── -->
<div id="reporting" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">05</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Reporting, Websites,<br><em>and a First Engagement</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Firms often can't see their own numbers without someone running a report by hand. I build
        dashboards, which are single screens of live charts and tables, and automated reports that
        build themselves on a schedule, from the data you already have. The
        <a href="/dashboard-gallery/">dashboard gallery</a> includes a sample legal billing
        dashboard. It uses made-up data and is there to show what's possible, not to show any real
        firm's numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also build websites for law firms: attorney profiles, practice-area pages, a blog, and a
        CMS (the editing area behind the site) that your own staff can update, with training and
        handoff documentation. See
        <a href="/small-business-website-design-wichita/">small business website design in Wichita</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A first engagement starts with listening. I interview a few of your staff who hold
        different job titles, to find where workflow pain points concentrate. Then I write an
        outline of where I think the inefficiencies sit, along with some possible solutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every conversation starts with a free scoping call. Pricing for the different kinds of work
        is on the <a href="/services/">Services page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── CTA STRIP ── -->
<div class="cta-strip">
  <div class="cta-glow"></div>
  <div class="cta-eyebrow">// Start Here</div>
  <h2 class="cta-headline">Let's Look at<br><em>Your Firm's Tech.</em></h2>
  <p class="cta-sub">
    Tell me about your firm's technology headaches. Every engagement starts with a free
    scoping call.
  </p>
  <div class="cta-actions">
    <a href="/contact/" class="btn-primary">Book a Free Scoping Call</a>
    <a href="/services/" class="btn-ghost">See Services &amp; Pricing</a>
  </div>
</div>

</Layout>
